00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:45.830
and how can I play apply machine
learning to automatically look

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:46.820
for those frames?

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:51.820
Hopefully this is useful.

00:15:51.820 --> 00:15:54.480
Gives an overview of
what we do intelligence.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:59.080
Let me switch to another
demo that highlights

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:01.380
face recognition and detection.

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:15.100
Here is a demo, where
we apply in addition

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:20.010
to Video Intelligence
API, and also Vision API.

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:23.440
Find in the frame, if
there is a face present.

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:26.290
And if it is a frame
that I can recognize,

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:31.220
find some summary from,
in this case, wiki page.

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:33.800
Let me quickly walk
through the pipeline

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:37.340
and highlight, or
showcase, the demo

00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:38.740
and I'll play the video file.

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:50.780
So here is the pipeline that
looks from a demo standpoint.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:54.190
So we have raw video file.

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:58.180
We process that using the
Video Intelligence API.

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:01.720
The Video Intelligence
API provides coordinates

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:04.660
and face-related
coordinates, again,

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:07.089
from each frame
standpoint-- meaning,

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:08.950
if there is a face present--

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:12.819
and coordinates and the
frame details of the effort.

00:17:12.819 --> 00:17:16.599
Then what we do is we
pass it to the Vision API

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:21.200
and find if it is a face
that we can recognize.

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:24.790
And if we are able to
recognize the face,

00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:29.060
can I get some summary
details from Wikipedia?

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:33.490
So from a pipeline standpoint,
multiple APIs are involved,

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:38.500
and the data pipeline makes the
integration here much easier,

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:44.750
because all we are doing here
is a couple of API integrations.

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:48.010
So let me play the
video for a few seconds,

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:49.727
and I'll add some commentary.

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:54.281
SPEAKER 4: The
Tyrell Corporation.

00:17:57.508 --> 00:17:59.766
SPEAKER 5: Mr Decker,
Dr. Elden Tyrell.

00:17:59.766 --> 00:18:01.190
SPEAKER 6: I don't
get it, Tyrell.

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:04.340
SPEAKER 7: Commerce is
our goal here at Tyrell.

00:18:04.340 --> 00:18:08.320
More human than
human is our motto.

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:13.790
PRABHU PALANISAMY: So you can
see here, as the video plays,

00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:16.700
we are able to use
the functionality

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:18.710
of the intelligence
the API, split

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:20.900
the video into multiple frames.

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:24.320
And within the frame,
we are looking for faces

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:26.360
that the system can recognize.

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:29.520
And as you can see here, the
moment a face is recognized,

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:32.560
we are able to take
a snapshot out of it,

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:34.550
pass it through the Vision API.

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:38.390
And if it is a celebrity,
we can find the details

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:41.930
from the results that are
provided from the Vision API.

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:44.960
And we are able to make
a call to Wikipedia API

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:46.920
to summarize the results.

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:49.100
So now, again, for
functionalities

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.740
for customers who
are looking for face

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:56.210
detection and other
location-based intelligence,

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:59.720
these types of use
cases are much easier

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:03.770
to implement with the Google
Cloud framework as well

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:04.700
as the APIs.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:17.880
Lastly, I'm going to
highlight demo of Vision API

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:21.100
and how to do
document processing.

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:24.930
So if we look at
it, there are a lot

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:27.720
of documents that
are structure space.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:29.460
Like, it could be
an invoice document,

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:31.040
it could be a purchase order.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:33.570
There are a lot of
structural documents

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:37.600
that require human
as well as other

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:41.460
workflow-based integrations
to either insert it

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:45.400
into a back end system
for processing, et cetera.

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:49.640
What if we can use
Google Vision API

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:52.710
and see if we can
automate such processes?

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:55.250
For example, in
this demo here, let

00:19:55.250 --> 00:20:01.430
me showcase how an
invoice can automatically

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:04.760
extract the key fields
using the Vision API

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:07.850
and, say, integrated
to a Salesforce system.

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:13.070
This will be useful for users
and avoid hand typing or use

00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:16.130
of other OCR technologies
from an external integration

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:17.960
standpoint.

00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:18.835
So if you look here--

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:25.040
let me start with the
template structure document.

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:27.840
So in this case, it's
a structured template

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:31.530
and can be used
Vision API to extract

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:34.980
some of the key fields-- in this
case name, address, and date,

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:35.480
et cetera.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:40.420
From a processing
standpoint, what happens

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:43.450
here is this
particular document is

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:47.302
being processed through Vision
API, and we look for entities.

00:20:47.302 --> 00:20:48.760
Like say, is there
a named present?

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:49.980
Is there a date present?

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:53.560
And with the combination of
rules based, coordinates based,

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:59.200
and entities based, we can
extract key fields easy.

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:04.810
The same concept if I apply
it to an invoice document.

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:06.760
It's a structured document.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:10.160
It has certain key fields
that I need to look for, say,

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:14.590
for example, invoice date,
account number, invoice amount,

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:17.750
description, et cetera.

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:20.030
We follow the same
pipeline process,

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:23.530
where we can pass it
to the Vision API.

00:21:23.530 --> 00:21:25.680
And now the Vision
API can quickly

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:28.340
give me the results of it.

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:31.280
Now that if I'm able to
extract these entities,

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:34.010
I can integrate to
my back end system.

00:21:34.010 --> 00:21:38.272
For example, send this to
a Salesforce application.

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:44.140
Hopefully, that gives
you the overview

00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:49.180
of how to process image files,
video files, and applying

00:21:49.180 --> 00:21:51.270
Vision for OCR-type use cases.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:58.110
Let me switch back
to my slide and cover

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:01.660
a couple of lessons
learned, best practices

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:04.450
of some of the
considerations based

00:22:04.450 --> 00:22:06.730
on our recent implementations.

00:22:06.730 --> 00:22:09.310
As you can see on
the top, if we were

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:13.450
to look at machine learning
for unstructured data sets,

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:15.780
there are a variety
of use cases here.

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:20.720
We can apply APIs.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:23.740
We can apply new
functionality that Google

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:29.080
released called AutoML, useful
for custom training models.

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:33.120
Similarly, there are
ML kits now available,

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:37.480
makes that mobile application
development much easier.

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:42.130
Then the super
developer-centric tensor

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:45.040
flow with a large
community following,

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:49.450
where we can build and deploy
custom machine learning models.

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:53.110
In addition to it, there are
also other open source tools,

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:59.920
and community has built a lot
of machine learning libraries

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:02.502
for use and integration.

00:23:02.502 --> 00:23:06.190
So if we look at it, there are
a lot of options available.

00:23:06.190 --> 00:23:09.010
There are API, there are
models, there are open source

00:23:09.010 --> 00:23:11.390
frameworks, et cetera.

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:14.150
Some of the considerations
we have seen

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:18.590
is one, defining the user
stories, or requirements.

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:21.830
This is no different from
any of the other project

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:24.470
development or implementation.

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:26.420
Care should be
taken to make sure

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:30.020
that who the user is,
what type of profile,

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:33.260
what's the business value from
an implementation standpoint,

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:37.310
and some of the other
infrastructure-related use

00:23:37.310 --> 00:23:38.010
cases.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:40.100
For example,
authentication, security,

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:43.070
performance, how the error
handling should be applied,

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:45.170
et cetera.

00:23:45.170 --> 00:23:47.840
This is where
SpringML can provide

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:50.660
the right guidance and
the implementation support

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:53.710
for all the enterprises.

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:58.100
Similarly, if we
look at it, data

00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:05.800
is the core element in deploying
machine learning projects.

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:09.280
So particularly in this
case for media research,

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:10.760
data files are large.

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:14.800
So how do we come up
with a data pipeline

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:16.420
from an integration standpoint?

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:17.710
Where do we store the data?

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:19.370
How do we store the data?

00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:24.250
What type of structure that will
help from an analysis of these

00:24:24.250 --> 00:24:26.880
files?

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:28.820
Then, finally, the integration.

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:31.730
So, for example, if
I have a media file,

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:34.880
I analyze the media file,
the machine learning works,

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:36.140
now what?

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:39.530
How does this particular
machine learning insight

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:44.540
get integrated to the
existing business uses?

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:46.610
Integration needs to
be thought through

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:51.680
to make sure that the insights
that are derived are useful

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:54.170
and customers can make
informed decisions

00:24:54.170 --> 00:24:55.730
based on these insights.

00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:58.340
And, finally, migration--
how am I going

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:00.500
to process historical data?

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:05.240
How am I going to make
sure that the past insights

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:07.505
and the new machine
learning model can coexist?

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:13.220
The last piece is once
I have the user stories,

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:16.190
we find the business
alignment is the--

00:25:16.190 --> 00:25:21.410
when I have my data
side, the technology team

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:24.980
is in alignment, how the
automation piece of it?

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:28.640
At SpringML, we joke that
machine learning development

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:32.340
is easy, but automation
is super hard.

00:25:32.340 --> 00:25:35.820
So how are we going to deploy
these machine learning models,

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:39.710
automate them, and
make the DevOps easier?

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:43.280
Again, Google has come up with
some of the framework that

00:25:43.280 --> 00:25:44.090
makes it easy.

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:46.910
For example, for tensor
flow model development,

00:25:46.910 --> 00:25:48.980
there is a framework
called cube flow, which

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:51.980
uses the Kubernetes
container model to make

00:25:51.980 --> 00:25:54.120
DevOps much easier.

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:56.820
So from an implementation
standpoint,

00:25:56.820 --> 00:26:00.140
we need to make sure that
the user stories are defined,

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:04.830
the data side discovered, and
then the automation side of it.

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:07.050
Hopefully that gives
an insight into some

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:10.160
of the lessons learned.

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:17.239
Now, stay tuned for Live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:26:17.239 --> 00:26:17.739
minute.

00:30:06.281 --> 00:30:08.390
ELIAS PINTO: And we're back.

00:30:08.390 --> 00:30:12.450
Well thank you, Prabhu, for that
presentation and demonstration.

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:14.730
So during the
demonstration, we had

00:30:14.730 --> 00:30:18.330
some folks submit some
questions from the audience.

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:20.910
So we wanted to present
some questions to you.

00:30:20.910 --> 00:30:23.740
And the first one here--

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:27.060
how long does it
take to implement

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:28.440
one of these projects?

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:30.497
Is there an average duration?

00:30:30.497 --> 00:30:31.830
What can you tell us about that?

00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:34.790
PRABHU PALANISAMY: Sure, that's
a very good question, Elias,

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:38.000
where we get that from
our customers often.

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:41.940
There is a lot of interest
on machine learning.

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:45.430
There is obviously a lot of
hype associated with it as well.

00:30:45.430 --> 00:30:48.720
But when it comes
to implementation,

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:51.420
historically, given
the complexity

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:55.680
and the implementation
duration, customers

00:30:55.680 --> 00:31:00.780
fear that it may be
intensive from a resource

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:05.890
standpoint with very minimal
results from the product.

00:31:05.890 --> 00:31:08.580
So what we take
through our customers

00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:15.510
is taking these projects
in sprint-based, agile,

00:31:15.510 --> 00:31:18.270
incremental delivery model.

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:20.160
We want to make
sure we understand

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:21.480
the vision of the customer.

00:31:21.480 --> 00:31:25.770
We want to make sure we
understand the long-term goal

00:31:25.770 --> 00:31:30.900
and how the customer wants
to derive insights out of it.

00:31:30.900 --> 00:31:34.720
Then we break it down
into smaller pieces.

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:39.900
For example, one of the things
at SpringML is if we cannot

00:31:39.900 --> 00:31:43.710
show value of a machine
learning project in six weeks,

00:31:43.710 --> 00:31:46.440
then we are not defining
the [? version ?] properly.

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:49.530
So the demos and the
projects that I showed

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:51.930
during the session have--

00:31:51.930 --> 00:31:53.880
most of those projects
were completed

00:31:53.880 --> 00:31:56.100
in six to eight weeks.

00:31:56.100 --> 00:31:59.280
That doesn't mean that
we're simplifying it.

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:02.910
But at the same
time, these projects

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:07.980
doesn't have to be implemented
in a traditional multi-month

00:32:07.980 --> 00:32:09.480
implementation.

00:32:09.480 --> 00:32:13.320
So we make sure we
understand the vision

00:32:13.320 --> 00:32:17.100
and come up with an
implementation plan that

00:32:17.100 --> 00:32:20.520
comprises of multiple
sprints, and shows

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:24.600
value in each and every
sprint schedule, et cetera, so

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:27.485
that way customers
can take that insight,

00:32:27.485 --> 00:32:29.980
adapt it to the
business process,

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:32.330
let the feedback loop come in--

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:34.680
meaning, what are the
business users saying?

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:36.480
Are they finding value in it?

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:37.950
What are the
suggestions that they

00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:42.330
are providing from the changes
to the machine learning model?

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:45.000
So we make sure that the
feedback loop comes back

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:47.120
again to the next
sprint schedule and make

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:48.960
sure the machine
learning models that we

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:52.752
built are to the customer
requirements and expectations.

00:32:52.752 --> 00:32:53.710
ELIAS PINTO: Thank you.

00:32:53.710 --> 00:32:55.230
Thank you.

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:56.620
We have a couple more.

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:04.270
So an audience member said,
I'm already in another cloud,

00:33:04.270 --> 00:33:07.535
can I still use machine
learning on Google Cloud?

00:33:07.535 --> 00:33:08.680
PRABHU PALANISAMY: Perfect.

00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:11.540
This is, again,
a great question.

00:33:11.540 --> 00:33:14.320
Customers, particularly
large enterprises,

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:17.740
have a variety of
deployment methods.

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:22.690
And if you look at the
Google Cloud machine learning

00:33:22.690 --> 00:33:27.370
tool sets, as I highlighted,
some of the tool sets

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:32.410
are API-based that can be
moved from multiple systems.

00:33:32.410 --> 00:33:35.950
Some of the technologies
are open source,

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:37.810
for example TensorFlow.

00:33:37.810 --> 00:33:42.410
And from a machine learning
implementation standpoint,

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:46.531
depending on the assets, there
are also hardware dependency

00:33:46.531 --> 00:33:47.030
to it.

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:50.380
For example, Google has
a TPU-based framework

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:54.300
to process large data sets,
image files, et cetera.

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:58.195
Google Cloud also
has GPUs to process

00:33:58.195 --> 00:33:59.860
data-intensive industries.

00:33:59.860 --> 00:34:04.030
And if we look at it, that's
the foundation of Kubernetes

00:34:04.030 --> 00:34:05.040
to a certain extent.

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:08.090
So there you have containers,
and you build your application,

00:34:08.090 --> 00:34:10.480
deploy it in Kubernetes,
and depending

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:12.820
on the platform or
the cloud of choice,

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:14.710
customer always has options.

00:34:14.710 --> 00:34:17.230
So from our
implementation standpoint,

00:34:17.230 --> 00:34:22.239
we make sure the customer gets
the advantages of the Google

00:34:22.239 --> 00:34:25.719
Cloud machine learning
APIs and leave the option

00:34:25.719 --> 00:34:29.420
to the infrastructure team to
the deployment aspect of it.

00:34:29.420 --> 00:34:30.730
ELIAS PINTO: Got it.

00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:32.199
OK.

00:34:32.199 --> 00:34:34.159
Couple more.

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:38.469
So what type of
resources/training

00:34:38.469 --> 00:34:40.090
do I need on my team?

00:34:40.090 --> 00:34:41.500
Sounds like somebody
is wondering

00:34:41.500 --> 00:34:43.690
if they have to go get a PhD.

00:34:43.690 --> 00:34:45.730
So what kind of
resources/training

00:34:45.730 --> 00:34:49.120
do I need on my team to
do the machine learning?

00:34:49.120 --> 00:34:50.500
PRABHU PALANISAMY: Perfect.

00:34:50.500 --> 00:34:53.590
So from a project
implementation standpoint,

00:34:53.590 --> 00:34:56.679
this is, again, people
fear that I need a PhD

00:34:56.679 --> 00:35:01.390
or I need a data scientist, or I
need some exceptional resources

00:35:01.390 --> 00:35:05.080
that are far and
few as cost to find.

00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:08.640
If you look at it from a machine
learning project, or data

00:35:08.640 --> 00:35:13.750
analytics standpoint, the need
for machine learning expertise

00:35:13.750 --> 00:35:18.220
is probably 10% to 15% of
the whole project itself.

00:35:18.220 --> 00:35:19.980
So that's the core piece--

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:24.110
a core resource needed, but
it is from the larger context

00:35:24.110 --> 00:35:27.460
standpoint, it's a small
slice in the implementation.

00:35:27.460 --> 00:35:30.820
If we look at it
in our projects,

00:35:30.820 --> 00:35:34.390
the data integration tend
to be much longer and much

00:35:34.390 --> 00:35:37.710
labor intensive than actual
machine learning development

00:35:37.710 --> 00:35:38.560
itself.

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:43.270
And as folks know, the
exercise of data cleaning,

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:49.330
data wrangling, data
processing oftentimes

00:35:49.330 --> 00:35:51.530
is much more than
actually machine

00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:53.360
learning development itself.

00:35:53.360 --> 00:35:55.630
So from a resource
standpoint, some

00:35:55.630 --> 00:35:59.980
of the project-related
governance have to be there.

00:35:59.980 --> 00:36:03.350
So you need a strong project
manager, a business analyst,

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:06.190
a subject matter expertise
that the customer

00:36:06.190 --> 00:36:09.670
knows what is that insight.

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:13.270
And from an implementation
technology standpoint,

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:16.630
obviously a data scientist
and a data engineer

00:36:16.630 --> 00:36:18.130
are common resources.

00:36:18.130 --> 00:36:22.330
And, particularly, Google
Cloud has done a phenomenal job

00:36:22.330 --> 00:36:27.640
on the technology
information backcasting.

00:36:27.640 --> 00:36:30.740
For example, I'm a big fan
of Cloud OnAir myself, where

00:36:30.740 --> 00:36:33.720
I have learned about
Kubernetes, [? MLKs, ?]

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:36.880
the new technologies
that are being deployed

00:36:36.880 --> 00:36:38.270
from Google's standpoint.

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:41.290
So from the feedback
prospective,

00:36:41.290 --> 00:36:45.760
resources-wise, I would consider
looking from a holistic project

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:46.760
standpoint.

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:49.690
So as much as you need the
data scientists and the data

00:36:49.690 --> 00:36:52.840
engineer, you also need to
have a project manager who

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:55.480
understands what went through
from a machine learning

00:36:55.480 --> 00:36:56.320
standpoint.

00:36:56.320 --> 00:36:59.260
And also a subject matter
expert, business analysts,

00:36:59.260 --> 00:37:02.867
who can communicate the business
users to the data scientists

00:37:02.867 --> 00:37:03.450
and engineers.

00:37:03.450 --> 00:37:05.180
ELIAS PINTO: Good, I
thought I had to get a PhD.

00:37:05.180 --> 00:37:05.530
Good.

00:37:05.530 --> 00:37:05.880
PRABHU PALANISAMY: [LAUGHS]

00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:06.838
ELIAS PINTO: Thank you.

00:37:06.838 --> 00:37:08.350
We have a last--

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:09.820
seems like to be
the last question.

00:37:09.820 --> 00:37:14.830
So how do you scale the
processes end to end?

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:23.870
PRABHU PALANISAMY: Yes this
is, again, one of the areas

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:30.080
where a probably project team
or the implementation team

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:32.060
does not see it through the end.

00:37:32.060 --> 00:37:35.720
Like I mentioned, oftentimes
machine learning development

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:39.220
is easy, but the
operationalizing part of it

00:37:39.220 --> 00:37:40.650
is super hard.

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:43.530
So from the
processes standpoint,

00:37:43.530 --> 00:37:45.560
how do you scale end to end?

00:37:45.560 --> 00:37:48.980
So the infrastructure side of
it needs to be thought through.

00:37:48.980 --> 00:37:52.650
And also from the business
side of it, as I said,

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:54.770
that feedback loop
is very important.

00:37:54.770 --> 00:37:55.730
Am I seeing value?

00:37:55.730 --> 00:37:57.960
Am I deriving the
value out of it?

00:37:57.960 --> 00:38:02.860
And, accordingly, make the
production deployment elastic.

00:38:02.860 --> 00:38:05.900
Today, with the
introduction of containers,

00:38:05.900 --> 00:38:09.530
I don't need to have a scale
of processing 100 nodes all

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:10.400
the time.

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:13.060
You come up with a
model, you deploy it.

00:38:13.060 --> 00:38:16.450
If the business users like it,
if the customers are liking it,

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:19.770
they are deriving the value,
scale on a need basis.

00:38:19.770 --> 00:38:22.160
And with the
Kubernetes cube flow

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:25.670
and some of the
container-based implementation,

00:38:25.670 --> 00:38:27.200
it makes it easier.

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:28.800
So you can start small.

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:32.570
For example, some of the
projects that I showcased often

00:38:32.570 --> 00:38:34.160
has large data sets.

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:36.220
For example, a
three-hour tennis video

00:38:36.220 --> 00:38:38.780
quickly run into [INAUDIBLE],,
similarly, other media

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:39.600
companies.

00:38:39.600 --> 00:38:44.750
So if we were a set up on
infrastructure on the get-go,

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:46.760
it may look monumental.

00:38:46.760 --> 00:38:52.070
Rather, we can start small,
scale, and use the Google Cloud

00:38:52.070 --> 00:38:52.860
functionalities.

00:38:52.860 --> 00:38:56.450
Most of the Google
Cloud product suites--

00:38:56.450 --> 00:39:00.020
for example, App
Engine, Kubernetes--

00:39:00.020 --> 00:39:02.240
allow to do elastic scaling.

00:39:02.240 --> 00:39:03.860
Make sure that
infrastructure team

00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:06.640
is aware of it and
the configuration set

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:10.430
in a way you can start small,
scale based on feedback.

00:39:10.430 --> 00:39:13.250
And, obviously, with Google
Cloud, some of the resources

00:39:13.250 --> 00:39:14.570
are infinite scale.

00:39:14.570 --> 00:39:16.580
ELIAS PINTO: So we built
a data center already.

00:39:16.580 --> 00:39:18.080
You don't need to
build another one.

00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:18.620
PRABHU PALANISAMY: Excellent.

00:39:18.620 --> 00:39:20.210
ELIAS PINTO: Excellent.

00:39:20.210 --> 00:39:22.620
I think that's all the
questions that we have today.

00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:25.380
So for the folks online,
please stay tuned.

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:27.440
We have another
session coming up,

00:39:27.440 --> 00:39:31.490
and it's titled "99
Problems but GPU Ain't One."

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:32.630
Sounds like a fun one.

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:35.930
So, again, my name is
Elias and Prabhu here.

00:39:35.930 --> 00:39:38.870
And we thank you for your
participation with questions.

00:39:38.870 --> 00:39:40.040
And until next time.

